 ### Fast Calculation of Local Vicinity Information
-An incremental algorithm to calculate the mean and standard deviation of an $n \times n \times n$ region around each voxel in $O(1)$ time, assuming CT values follow a normal distribution [S2]. It uses incremental updates for Sum1D, Sum2D, and Sum3D to compute averages and variance efficiently [S2]. Region size $n$ is typically 15 [S2].
+An incremental algorithm to calculate the mean and standard deviation of an $n \times n \times n$ region around each voxel in $O(1)$ time, assuming CT values follow a normal distribution [S2]. It uses incremental updates for Sum1D, Sum2D, and Sum3D to compute averages and variance efficiently [S2]. Region size $n$ is typically 15 [S2]. The incremental updates follow:
+- Sum1D(s, t + 1, u) = Sum1D(s, t, u) + Head1D − Tail1D, where Head1D = voxels_{s + n/2, t, u} and Tail1D = voxels_{s - n/2, t, u} [S2].
+- Sum2D(s+1, t, u) = Sum2D(s, t, u) + Head2D − Tail2D, where Head2D = Sum1D_{s, t + n/2, u} and Tail2D = Sum1D_{s, t - n/2, u} [S2].
+- Sum3D(s, t, u + 1) is calculated in $O(1)$ using the same incremental logic [S2].
+- Avg3D(s, t, u) = Sum3D(s, t, u) / n^3 [S2].
+- σ^2(x) = SquaredAvg3D(s, t, u) − Avg3D(s, t, u)^2, where SquaredAvg3D is obtained by repeating the Sum1D/Sum2D/Sum3D procedure on a volume of squared CT values [S2].
 
